60 days ago today on this very spot a  young man petitioned the FEC for  permission to form a super PAC ladies  and gentlemen I'm sorry to say we won  everybody in there brother even Stephen  Colbert is freaking out about super PACs  an outgrowth of the Citizens United  decision in 2010 traditional political  action committees are subject to federal  limits on how much money donors can give  in election cycles super PACs allow  groups such as nonprofit corporations or  unions to spend unlimited money on  political speech as long as they don't  coordinate their activity with the  official campaign of a candidate but for  all the bellyaching here are three good  reasons not to get worked up over super  PACs in the wake of an anti Mitt Romney  documentary from winning our future a  group tied to billionaire sheldon  adelson the New York Times shredded that  the film underscores how citizens united  has made it possible for a wealthy  individual to influence an election  actually it's always been legal for rich  people to spend what they want as long  as they make independent expenditures  that aren't coordinated with official  campaigns billionaires don't need super  PACs to get their message out but super  PACs may just let the rest of us have  our say Senator John McCain's campaign  finance legislation was rendered mostly  moot by citizens united complains that  super PACs not only flood elections with  money but flooded with negative messages  McCain who lost his run for the  presidency admits that negative  campaigning works but he doesn't like  the tone yet study after study shows not  only that negative advertising works  with voters but that negative ads  actually contain more information than  gauzy pay ins to America and the virtues  of the candidates who pay for those ads  there's no question that super PACs aim  to benefit some candidates by taking aim  at others adel's in the money bags  behind the anti Romney documentary is  known to be a newt gingrich fan but as  long as super PACs don't coordinate with  candidates or official party  apparatchiks they take messaging out of  the hands of party leaders and spread it  around elsewhere in a way that has got  to be more representative of more views  of more voters super PACs are just the  latest Kaz's Bella and push for  controlling specifically political  speech in the name of making elections  fair there's no doubt that there are  loophole arising from the last round of  campaign finance reform in the attempt  to limit the amount of money politicians  would have to raise to get their  messages out but it's time to recognize  that the only way to stop creating new  loopholes is by ending the always in  effect of laws designed to lower the  costs office seekers need to spend to  buy our votes for reason TV I'm Nick  Gillespie
 

